# AGENTS.local.md - machine-local instruction overlay (example)

Copy this file to `AGENTS.local.md` beside it and fill in what applies to this
checkout. `AGENTS.local.md` is gitignored: never commit it, never put
credentials or key material in it, and never move a rule into it that another
checkout also needs.

The instruction loader reads it after the base files, so it can refine or
override them for this machine. It is the right home for:

- host names, addresses and SSH details for build or test machines;
- local paths, profile directories and toolchain locations;
- environment quirks specific to this box;
- anything else that identifies this checkout or its operator.

## Windows build host (example)

The dedicated Windows host is reachable over key-only SSH as `<user>@<host>`,
using `<path-to-private-key>` with `IdentitiesOnly=yes`, `BatchMode=yes`, and
`StrictHostKeyChecking=yes`. It is a manually driven build host, not a CI
runner. The account is a standard user, so resolve its registered profile
explicitly rather than assuming it matches the account name; the checkout and
the package manager live under that profile.

Keep private keys and signing credentials out of transfers. Compare both working
trees before transferring changes - the Windows checkout may hold an earlier
snapshot of uncommitted work, and other sessions may be editing Linux
concurrently. Consult the Windows test runbook before interpreting probe results
or rebuilding the installer.

## Local environment (example)

Record any local constraint that changes how checks should be run - a small
temporary filesystem, a restricted sandbox, a specific toolchain location. Keep
the portable rule in the tracked instruction files and put only the
machine-specific value here.
